A student laptop has a harder life than most. It's carried to class every day, used for eight hours at a stretch, and expected to last three or four years of a course — on a budget that is usually set by parents or a first internship stipend. The good news is that most students need far less computing power than laptop marketing suggests. The trick is knowing where your course actually needs performance and where it doesn't.

## Step 1: Start with what your course needs

Different courses put very different demands on a laptop. Use this as a starting point:

| Course or use | Processor | RAM | Storage | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| School, commerce, arts, humanities | Core i3 / i5 (8th gen or newer) | 8GB | 256GB SSD | Portability and battery matter most |
| Management, law, general college use | Core i5 (8th gen or newer) | 8–16GB | 256–512GB SSD | Many tabs, documents and video calls |
| Engineering and computer science | Core i5 / i7 (10th gen or newer) | 16GB | 512GB SSD | Programming, virtual machines, simulation tools |
| Architecture, design, video editing | Core i7 or Ryzen 7 with dedicated graphics | 16GB+ | 512GB+ SSD | Check your software's published requirements |
| Data science, machine learning | Core i7 / Ryzen 7 | 16–32GB | 512GB+ SSD | Heavy training is usually done on cloud or lab machines |

Before buying, **check your college's or software vendor's stated requirements**. Tools like AutoCAD, SolidWorks, MATLAB or Adobe apps publish minimum and recommended specs, and some departments issue their own lists. Meeting the recommended spec is safer than meeting the minimum.

## Step 2: Prioritise the specs that matter

### An SSD is non-negotiable

A laptop with a solid-state drive boots in seconds and opens apps quickly. A hard drive makes even a powerful processor feel slow. Every laptop worth buying for study today should have an SSD.

### RAM: 8GB minimum, 16GB if you can

8GB handles documents, browsing and online classes. If you code, use design tools or keep dozens of tabs open, 16GB makes a real difference. Many business laptops have a free RAM slot, so starting at 8GB and upgrading later is often cheap.

### Processor generation over model number

A newer Core i5 is often faster than an older Core i7. Aim for Intel 8th generation or newer — this also matters because [Windows 11 officially supports](/blog/best-refurbished-laptops-under-30000) processors from roughly this generation onwards, which keeps security updates coming.

### Battery life

Students often can't find a free plug in lecture halls or libraries. Look at the measured battery health on refurbished listings — a healthy battery in an efficient laptop can comfortably get through several hours of notes and browsing. Here's [how to check battery health](/blog/how-to-check-laptop-battery-health) yourself.

## Step 3: Think about size and weight

A 14-inch laptop is the sweet spot for most students: large enough to work on comfortably, small enough for a backpack. 15.6-inch models give you a bigger screen and often a number pad (useful for commerce and engineering students) but are heavier to carry all day. 13-inch models are the lightest but can feel cramped for long sessions.

## Step 4: Decide on Windows or macOS

- **Windows laptops** offer the widest range of prices and models, and support almost all engineering and specialist academic software.
- **MacBooks** are excellent for writing, design and general study, with strong battery life on Apple silicon models. Check first that any required course software runs on macOS — some engineering tools are Windows-only.

Browse [refurbished MacBooks](/category/macbooks) or [business laptops](/category/business-laptops) to compare.

## Step 5: Set a realistic budget

Here's roughly how budgets map to use in the refurbished market:

- **Under ₹20,000:** capable for school work, online classes and office apps. Expect older-generation Core i3/i5 business laptops — check processor generation carefully. [Laptops under ₹20,000](/laptops/under-20000).
- **₹20,000–₹30,000:** the best-value range for most college students — 8th–11th gen Core i5, 8–16GB RAM, SSD. [Laptops under ₹30,000](/laptops/under-30000).
- **₹30,000–₹50,000:** newer processors, 16GB RAM, better screens, some MacBooks and laptops with dedicated graphics. [Laptops under ₹50,000](/laptops/under-50000).

## Why refurbished works well for students

Refurbished business laptops are built for daily travel and heavy use — sturdy hinges, good keyboards and easy-to-replace parts — which is exactly what a student needs. Because they cost less than an equivalent new laptop, the savings can go towards a RAM upgrade, a warranty extension or course books.

The key is buying from a seller that tests every unit, discloses battery health and cosmetic grade, and offers a real warranty and return window. Read more about [the difference between refurbished and used](/blog/refurbished-vs-used-laptop-difference).

## Quick checklist before you buy

1. Does it meet your course's recommended software requirements?
2. Is the processor 8th generation or newer?
3. At least 8GB RAM — and can it be upgraded?
4. An SSD with enough space for your files?
5. What is the measured battery health?
6. Is the warranty period clearly stated?
7. Is a charger included?

If you tick all seven, you've found a laptop that will see you through your course. [Browse all refurbished laptops](/laptops).
